{"kind":"artifact","text":"Session identifiers no longer disclosed, unmerged on branch task/session-privacy at d4107613c1d7dfdd6fdebb41f2d9b3649202effe. The finding was correct and slightly worse than stated: a session identifier is not merely a name but a capability, since presenting one to POST /v0/say makes the service resolve it to an actor, load that actor's private key, and sign an ephemeral frame as them, while presenting one to DELETE /v0/presence/{session} ends that actor's lease, with no proof and no binding to the caller. The identifier was published in two places rather than one — as the keys of the GET /v0/presence map and as Change.ID on presence, renewal, departure, and expiry entries in the wait and watch streams — so any client that could read presence could speak as anyone present and silence them. Presence is now published under a one-way session handle derived from the identifier under its own domain tag, which preserves everything an observer legitimately needs, namely who is here, telling sessions apart, following a renewal and noticing a departure, while granting nothing; the identifier stays with the client that minted it. The tests are written as properties: no observable surface may contain an identifier, a handle must be stable and distinct and must not be accepted anywhere an identifier is required, one session holding only a handle cannot evict or speak for another, and a live session cannot be rebound to a different actor. The disclosure test was run against main first, where it fails by printing the identifier straight out of the snapshot, and against this head, where it passes. Two existing tests indexed presence by raw identifier and were adapted to the published key with every assertion intact, since what they actually check is unchanged. docs/reference.md now states what loopback still trusts: anything reaching the port can announce a session for any actor in local custody and speak as them, there is no authentication below that line by design, and on a machine with untrusted local users that boundary is the whole of the protection — while durable acts remain unaffected, signed from actor custody and verified by the fold. Verified against a running service that the announce is accepted, presence reports a handle, and the identifier appears in neither the presence response nor the watch stream. make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., and make build pass. Not reviewed and not merged.","body":{"commit":"d4107613c1d7dfdd6fdebb41f2d9b3649202effe","path":"."}}